---
title: AWS SigV4 signing locally
intent: Verify signatures using public fixture credentials and a local verifier.
group: security
features:
- signing
command: curlew run testapi/collections/50-signing.yaml --var mud="$MUDFLAT_URL" --var ws="$MUDFLAT_WS_URL" --var run="$RUN_ID"
order: 90
runnable: true
---

The credentials below are published test values accepted only by Mudflat. The fixture verifies AWS SigV4 and OAuth1 signatures. It never calls AWS and does not prove authorization to your AWS account. For real credentials use the manual’s authentication and redaction recipes.

## Prerequisites

Use the complete repository checkout and start Mudflat as described in the [cookbook setup](https://github.com/weiqigod/curlew/blob/main/site/README.md). Run these commands from the repository root with `curlew` on PATH.

```bash
export MUDFLAT_URL="${MUDFLAT_URL:-http://127.0.0.1:18080}"
export MUDFLAT_WS_URL="${MUDFLAT_WS_URL:-ws://127.0.0.1:18080}"
export RUN_ID="cookbook-$(date +%s)-$$"
```

## Run it

```bash
curlew run testapi/collections/50-signing.yaml --var mud="$MUDFLAT_URL" --var ws="$MUDFLAT_WS_URL" --var run="$RUN_ID"
```

A successful execution exits 0. Read assertion outcomes as well as the exit code.
No example output or timing numbers are fabricated here.

## Complete collection

<!-- cookbook-source: testapi/collections/50-signing.yaml -->
```yaml
# Request signing, verified by a server that recomputes the signature.
#
# Until this collection existed, internal/signer had emitted AWS SigV4 and
# OAuth 1.0a signatures for its whole life without a single one being checked
# by anything. Not a suspected defect — an absence of evidence.
#
# mudflat's verifier is written from the AWS documentation and RFC 5849 rather
# than from internal/signer, and its SigV4 side is pinned against AWS's own
# published get-vanilla test vector. So a failure here is a statement about
# curlew, not a coin toss between two unverified implementations.
name: Request signing, verified
description: |
  Signs requests with both registered schemes and asserts the server accepts
  them. On failure the response carries the canonical request, the string to
  sign and the base string, because a bare signature mismatch is not actionable.

variables:
  # Appendix B of the specification. Published and non-secret by design.
  aws_access_key: "AKIAMUDFLATTEST0000"
  aws_secret_key: "wJalrMudflatEXAMPLEKEY/K7MDENG/bPxRfi"
  oauth_consumer_key: "mud_consumer_0001"
  oauth_consumer_secret: "mud_consumer_secret_0001"
  oauth_token_secret: "mud_token_secret_0001"

requests:
  - name: aws sigv4 signature is accepted
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/verify/sigv4"
    signing:
      type: aws-sigv4
      params:
        region: us-east-1
        service: execute-api
        access_key: "{{aws_access_key}}"
        secret_key: "{{aws_secret_key}}"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      body:
        $.ok: { equals: true }
        $.algorithm: { equals: "AWS4-HMAC-SHA256" }
        # Every stage of the ladder, not just the final comparison.
        $.checks: { type: array }
      cel:
        - "response.body.checks.all(c, c.ok)"

  - name: sigv4 over a request with a query string
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/verify/sigv4?b=2&a=1&c=hello%20world"
    signing:
      type: aws-sigv4
      params:
        region: us-east-1
        service: execute-api
        access_key: "{{aws_access_key}}"
        secret_key: "{{aws_secret_key}}"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      body:
        # Canonical query ordering and %20-not-plus encoding are the two places
        # a SigV4 implementation most often diverges.
        $.ok: { equals: true }

  - name: sigv4 over a request with a body
    request:
      method: POST
      url: "{{mud}}/verify/sigv4"
      body:
        payload: "the signature covers the body hash"
    signing:
      type: aws-sigv4
      params:
        region: us-east-1
        service: execute-api
        access_key: "{{aws_access_key}}"
        secret_key: "{{aws_secret_key}}"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      body:
        $.ok: { equals: true }

  - name: an unsigned request is rejected with a usable message
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/verify/sigv4"
    assertions:
      status: 401
      body:
        $.ok: { equals: false }
        $.stage: { equals: "authorization_header" }
        $.hint: { contains: "aws-sigv4" }

  - name: oauth 1.0a signature is accepted
    request:
      method: POST
      url: "{{mud}}/verify/oauth1"
    signing:
      type: oauth1
      params:
        consumer_key: "{{oauth_consumer_key}}"
        consumer_secret: "{{oauth_consumer_secret}}"
        token_secret: "{{oauth_token_secret}}"
        nonce: "fixednonce"
        timestamp: "1700000000"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      body:
        $.ok: { equals: true }
        $.algorithm: { contains: "OAuth" }
        # Returned on success as well as failure: parameter normalisation is
        # invisible from outside, and seeing it is the only way to debug it.
        $.base_string: { contains: "oauth_consumer_key" }
```
